A||||15||Flamberge||The Five Fiero Families||||3||
Z||000000||Flamberge||10-29-2001||02:00 AM||||After being here for a few weeks, and lurking - but not taking part in - the flame wars of late, I have realized something about Fieros... there are five families of Fierophiles. (Say that 5 times hehehe)<P>We each are a part of at least 1 family, but may have a stake in the others as well... So here I go...<P>"Flamberge's Five Fiero Families"<P>1) Stock - These are the guys/gals that simply want to restore their Fieros, fixing the headlamp motors (*sigh*) and painting the engine louvres black again. They might replace the stereo, but probably not. The most they will do is tint the windows.<P>2) Custom - The people who belong to this family can't wait to redesign the body work, give it new rims/tires, and add cool side intakes. Dropping a new engine qualifies, but that is the attribute of a family below.<P>3) Racing - This is for the people who think that a 2700 lb. car that has good balance, a sleek shape, and enough space to put a V-8 in the Fiero, is good enough to race it. Quartermile times, 0-60's, and raw horsepower is focused on here. (Customs might drop a new engine in too, but they will most likely be as much or more concerned with how the engine looks (matching colors and liberal chroming.) <P>4) Kits - If you want to cut your Fiero in half so you can lengthen it and make it a "Lamborghini" you are in this family. You can take a Fiero, and when you are finished, the only people that recognise that the car was ever a Fiero are other Fiero-philes.<P>5) Contemporaries - This is a catchall family that covers people who focus mostly on customization for the sake of being "cool," or having a cruiser. Extreme wheel sizes, neon lights, sub-woofers, and "rice" paraphenalia would go here.<P>That's my list. Keep in mind that Most people here fall under multiple categories, and these are all archetypical just to illustrate a point. I for example, am mostly a Stock guy, but I would consider an engine change (3800 or maybe even V-8) and custom air intakes. The rest of the body I would leave stock, but neon lights are a possibility also, as is a new stereo. The only one of the above that I don't qualify for at all is the Kit family, but a lot of people do.<P>To each his own of course, but seeing how we differ and how we are alike has helped me understand the Fiero-phile psyche a little better. This is all my opinion, of course.<P>Thoughts?<BR><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||CoolBlue87GT||10-29-2001||07:51 AM||||Yes, that sounds like a reasonable cross section of the Fiero world. Good job.<P>I fit in the "Keep it stock & love it class"  I enjoy going to car shows and other get togethers with other owners. <P>When I had my 85 SE, the biggest modification I made was before even leaving the new car lot. I had the dealer remove the radio, & install the radio from my 84 coupe that I was trading in.<P>The 84 radio seemed much better then the 85.<P>Of course, I made one more, when I turned at a stop sign, right into the path of an oncomming phone pole, but that's another story. 8-(||||reg||6
Z||000002||Formula||10-29-2001||08:33 AM||||<BR>I have the cool body mods.. scoops, tint etc..<P>I have the high performance engine that isn't chromed out.  With dirty valve covers.<P>I have polished aluminum wheels.<P>I have a high wattage stereo with subwoofers.<P>I have neons, and strobes.<P>I have clear tailights.<P>ahhhhhhhhhh, I am a crossbreed, a Custom racing contemporary.  wait till I buy a stock yellow 88 gt that will not be touched and a ferrari 355 marenello kit! Then I'll have the fiero world covered.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||10-29-2001||08:50 AM||||somewhere bettween custom and contemporary i guess. there's no crowd i run with that i need to impress plus if being cool were the main goal i guess i'd start with another car. i like changing things and trying stuff that wasn't available when fieros came out or just wasn't used on them. i'd almost say i have more than one because you can only try so many ideas on one car.<P>i'd like to try at least one engine change (already took the engine out and gave it away) but i'm definitly not a racer. it's a combination of having had cars with more power and just being something i want to try. <P>i think for me the goal is to be able to say this car came to be as it is because i made it that way.  <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||10-29-2001||09:12 AM||||I'm a Custom ~ Racing all the way. No stock, no neons (ugh!), no kits...<P>However, the Racing category has sub-categories in it...<P>Drags and Carvers...<P>I'm not into 0-60 or 1/4 mile (although I will test those for posterity)...I'm into carving cones (autocross), so my mods must be carefully planned so-as not to put me into a "racemod" class, and I care more about handling than all-out power (did I say that?). OK, some power is necessary (like say, 240-340HP), but handling is still more important...<P>Cool list!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
